  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly book shows clear signs of public reach and ongoing use. It has been downloaded 3,926 times through OAPEN, appears in public knowledge ecosystems including Wikipedia-linked attention, and is present across commercial and reader-facing platforms. Although the citation record is still at zero, the available evidence points to visibility beyond specialist bibliographic channels and to sustained interest in the book\u2019s subject.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Japan\u2019s Book Donation to the University of Louvain is a research-led historical study with strong public visibility. The record shows 3,926 OAPEN downloads, indicating that the book has been actively accessed rather than merely catalogued. It is also represented in public-facing discovery and discussion environments, with substantial Wikipedia-linked attention and additional traces on platforms such as Goodreads, Amazon, and LibraryThing. Taken together, these signals suggest that the book has reached readers beyond its immediate scholarly niche.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work\u2019s open-access status through a hybrid model further supports circulation and reuse, and the presence of a peer-review record indicates formal scholarly vetting. Its subject matter, centered on the Japanese book donation to the University of Louvain and the wider historical and political context around that collection, aligns with the book\u2019s interdisciplinary framing across history, political science, law, and related fields. The available record therefore suggests both academic and public relevance, even though formal citation uptake has not yet accumulated.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Evidence of teaching adoption is limited to library holding visibility rather than explicit course-use data, so it is best to describe this as discoverability in academic libraries rather than confirmed classroom use. Similarly, there are no event records in the current digest, so the strongest documented impact comes from downloads and public attention rather than conferences or public programming.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The funding record notes support from KU Leuven, which is consistent with the book\u2019s institutional and archival context. Overall, the pattern is one of substantial readership, broad discoverability, and active public-facing attention, with a citation trail that remains early in development.</p>",
